{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:MassTheory.QBetaC2EqNegOneStructural","@type":"LeanModule","name":"MassTheory.QBetaC2EqNegOneStructural","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C2Structural.partial_ladder_N2_factor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C2Structural.partial_ladder_N2_factor","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c-2-eq-neg-one-structural.lean#L64-L77","doc":"**The ladder-ansatz form $(1 + c_2 u^2)$ with $c_2 = -1$.**\n\nThis is the rewriting that turns the operator-valued $N{=}2$\nfactor into the precision-ladder normal form `(1 + c_k · u^k)`,\nconfirming the $c_2 = -1$ identification independently of any\nnumerical $q_0$ evaluation.  Direct consequence of `c_2_value`\nabove. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C2Structural.c_2_value","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C2Structural.c_2_value","kind":"theorem","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c-2-eq-neg-one-structural.lean#L55-L62","doc":"**Corollary: $c_2 = -1$.**\n\nReading the $N{=}2$ factor as $1 + c_2 \\cdot u^2$ with\n$u = b \\cdot \\hbar_q^2$, the structural identity above forces\n$c_2 = -1$. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.C2Structural.c_2_eq_neg_one_structural","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.C2Structural.c_2_eq_neg_one_structural","kind":"theorem","line":44,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c-2-eq-neg-one-structural.lean#L44-L53","doc":"**Structural identity for the $N{=}2$ correction factor.**\n\nSetting $u := b \\cdot \\hbar_q^2$, the operator-valued $N{=}2$\ncorrection $(1 - b^2 \\cdot \\hbar_q^4)$ to $Q_\\beta$ is identically\n$(1 - u^2)$.  Pure ring identity, holds over any commutative\nring containing $b$ and $\\hbar_q$. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:q-beta-c-2-eq-neg-one-structural","label":"prop:q-beta-c-2-eq-neg-one-structural","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c-2-eq-neg-one-structural.md"}],"signatureChunk":0}]}